 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S446-011\", \"clauses\": {\"solve_problems_involving_addition_and_subtraction\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F08\", \"template\": \"Then {actor} {verb} {amount}.\", \"reasoning\": \"F08's verb domain is the generator's two choices, gives away or receives, so the story step is an addition or subtraction the learner must solve. The question families F04 and F05 ask for the resulting quantity.\"}, \"3_to_4_numbers\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F03\", \"template\": \"Evaluate the {expr_label}: {expr_str}.\", \"reasoning\": \"Every printed expr_str value in F03 has 3 or 4 numbers (for example 1 + 46 + 39 has 3 and 12 + 23 + 29 + 95 has 4). No printed value has 2 or 5 numbers.\"}, \"up_to_2_digits\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F12\", \"template\": \"{v} {obj}\", \"reasoning\": \"F12 draws v from the expression's operands, whose members run from 2 to 99, so every amount a learner reads is at most 2 digits. The F03 printed values also have operands of at most 2 digits.\"}, \"problems_involving_money\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F05\", \"template\": \"How much money does {actor} have now?\", \"reasoning\": \"F05 asks for a money quantity. Money amounts also appear in F13 and F14 through the peso sign, so money problems are producible.\"}}, \"failing_families\": [\"F04\", \"F05\", \"F08\", \"F10\"], \"failing_templates\": [\"How many {obj} does {actor} have now?\", \"How much money does {actor} have now?\", \"Then {actor} {verb} {amount}.\", \"{actor} starts with {unit}.\"], \"overall\": \"FAIL\", \"reasoning\": \"F08 and F10 are listed in appears_in for question_text, so their statement-form templates can appear where the learner reads the question, and a statement with no question asks nothing. The actor slot in F04, F05, F08 and F10, and the obj slot in F04 and F11/F12, are each drawn separately from _WP_ACTORS or _WP_OBJECTS with no shared domain and no value_derivations link. A question can therefore name a different actor, object or money type from the story it follows, and the problem is unanswerable or misleading. F04 and F05 are fine in isolation but are at fault through this unlinked pairing. F01, F02, F03, F06, F07, F09, F13 and F14 read correctly on the printed values and domains, and no printed expression goes negative. F03 uses the word evaluate, which is not in cumulative_vocab; it is judged acceptable as an instruction verb, not as curriculum vocabulary.\"}",
